foundly important artery  this was an event there could have been  much more serious than the one i had and  throughout it all you have not lost your  sense of humor no no in fact i used to  do stand-up comedy  i made i made some good mileage out of  my heart attack i recently had a heart  attack  it was not fatal  making people laugh and laughing  yourself is just wonderful it's such a  release and you know it also keeps you  from taking yourself too seriously so  after your heart attack what kinds of  changes did you make in your life the  first changes i made were diet and  exercise and i've the biggest change  i've made you know in that regard is  that i don't look back and regret things  like  because regret is waste what i've  learned to do is to take  things that i cannot control and stop  obsessing over them stop worrying about  them i think i'm a lot less cynical than  i once was so you're training for a half  marathon  i'm now training for this half marathon  but there's nothing i can do about what  happened in the past so  except for learn the lessons of that  right and so that meant doing everything  i can  to prevent it from happening against you  know eating better staying in shape life  is a lot more fragile than you think  people would say you got to get some  sleep i don't sleep when i'm dead and  then when you like realize that maybe  you were closer to sleeping than you  knew  what do you hope that people who are  listening take away from this  conversation what i think is important  is that  you do your best to recognize what's  going on in your life and take charge of  it where you can  but just because i had a heart attack  doesn't mean  that it's all over in fact it's quite  the opposite you can come back from  problems and come back better and you  can do it without wallowing in regret or  if you watch me and you say  that could be me tomorrow then  then change  and that's what i hope people do  tara and andy are joining us now i could  have 